BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:12317110@fairmontstate.edu
DTSTAMP:20260803T173206Z
DTSTART:20260324T163000Z
DTEND:20260324T173000Z
SUMMARY:S-STEM Colloquium
DESCRIPTION:The second S-STEM Colloquium of the semester will be given by the Robert L. Carroll guest speaker\, Ricard Marsal. Ricard received his B.S. in Mathematics and Computer Science from Fairmont State University in 2021. The research presented in this talk was initiated during his senior year at Fairmont State. He is currently a PhD candidate in the Space Robotics Laboratory at the University of Luxembourg\, where his research focuses on robotic service operations using reinforcement learning. Title: How does research in space robotics work? This lecture explores the practical lifecycle of ML research for space robotics\, moving beyond theory into the "end-to-end" development pipeline. We will discuss the Simulation-First philosophy\, leveraging massively parallelized environments to accelerate agent learning\, and the engineering hurdles of the Sim2Real gap. By walking through the custom design\, construction\, and hardware-in-the-loop testing of robotic systems\, I will illustrate the day to day of a researcher in the multidisciplinary domain of robotics. Finally we will discuss future work and directions.
LOCATION:305 ET
URL:https://static.campuslabsengage.com/discovery/images/events/learning.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:12317121@fairmontstate.edu
DTSTAMP:20260803T173206Z
DTSTART:20260323T210000Z
DTEND:20260323T230000Z
SUMMARY:Robert L. Carroll Lecture 
DESCRIPTION:Reinforcement Learning (RL) has evolved from a theoretical framework into the backbone of modern autonomy\, powering everything from large language models to humanoid robotics and deep-space exploration. This lecture traces the path from the fundamentals of Markov Decision Processes (MDPs) to the deployment of sophisticated off-policy ActorCritic algorithms. Particular attention will be given to the intersection of RL and hypernetworksarchitectures that generate the weights of other networksto address multi-task Guidance\, Navigation\, and Control (GNC) problems for autonomous satellites. The talk will conclude with a discussion of the Sim2Real gap and strategies for transitioning models from high-fidelity simulations to real-world systems. Speaker Bio: Ricard Marsal received his B.S. in Mathematics and Computer Science from Fairmont State University in 2021. The research presented in this talk was initiated during his senior year at Fairmont State. He is currently a PhD candidate in the Space Robotics Laboratory at the University of Luxembourg\, where his research focuses on robotic service operations using reinforcement learning. About the Carroll Lectures:The Carroll Lecture Series\, co-sponsored by the Fairmont State Foundation and the Presidents Office\, is organized by the College of Science and Technology in honor of Dr. Robert L. Carroll (19101997). Dr. Carroll earned an A.B. in Education from Fairmont State College in 1933\, an M.S. in Mathematics from West Virginia University in 1940\, and a Ph.D. in Mathematical Physics from West Virginia University in 1944. Dr. Carroll served as Professor of Physics and head of the Physics Department at Fairmont State College from 1946 to 1956. His distinguished career also included positions as Associate Project Leader of Proximity Fuze Research with the National Bureau of Standards in Washington\, D.C.\; Chief Engineer and Dean of Academics at the U.S. Naval Test Pilot School in Patuxent River\, Maryland (where one of his students was Alan Shepard)\; and senior scientist and analyst with several government research and testing operations. From 1965 to 1977 he was head of the Department of Physics at Baptist College in Charleston\, South Carolina. His honors included listings in American Men of Science\, Whos Who in American Education\, Two Thousand Men of Achievement\, and the Ordnance Development Award for Naval Research.
LOCATION:305 ET
URL:https://static.campuslabsengage.com/discovery/images/events/learning.jpg
END:VEVENT
